{
  // Core scenes — everyday research, content and code-review workflows.
  "scenes": {
    "source_backed_paper": {
      "description": "Produce a paper or report grounded in official sources and verified citations.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when the input includes attached reports or files, researcher to gather authoritative sources, evidence_analyst to normalize the evidence ledger, paper_author to draft only from collected evidence, and source_verifier to check every citation and factual claim before finalizing.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "source_verifier",
        "researcher",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "outputStyle": {
          "description": "Requested style or citation format",
          "default": "technical report with inline references"
        }
      }
    },
    "verified_research_brief": {
      "description": "Produce a concise fact-checked research brief with named sources and explicit unresolved uncertainties.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when the request starts from attached material, researcher for broad source discovery and authoritative references, evidence_analyst to reconcile the evidence ledger, source_verifier to flag unsupported claims, and summarizer to produce the final brief with source mentions and open questions.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "source_verifier",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience for the brief",
          "default": "technical stakeholder"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How fresh the evidence needs to be",
          "default": "use the freshest reliable sources available and call out publication dates"
        }
      }
    },
    "deep_research": {
      "description": "Build a source-backed research dossier or design guide on a topic, with optional Mermaid visualizations. Used by the deep_research_packet and research_visual_digest jobs.",
      "task": "Run mission_coordinator. Phase 1 - researcher gathers authoritative sources and publishes structured share_finding records (topic, source title, URL, exact facts, caveats). Phase 2 - evidence_analyst reconciles the ledger and resolves conflicts. Phase 3 - paper_author drafts the dossier per the requested deliverable shape, then quality_supervisor runs one acceptance pass. Persist the final dossier with generate_document so it survives chat truncation.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Preferred output shape (dossier, design guide, comparison memo, etc.)",
          "default": "source-backed dossier with Mermaid diagrams where useful"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience for the dossier",
          "default": "decision maker"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How fresh the evidence needs to be",
          "default": "use the freshest reliable evidence available and call out publication dates"
        },
        "includeVisuals": {
          "description": "Whether to include Mermaid diagrams ('yes' or 'no')",
          "default": "yes"
        },
        "visualFocus": {
          "description": "Hint for what the visual should clarify",
          "default": "choose the clearest Mermaid visual structure for the topic"
        }
      }
    },
    "competitive_analysis": {
      "description": "Research and compare multiple competitors or products into a decision-oriented comparison brief.",
      "task": "Use mission_coordinator to partition the competitor research when needed, researcher to collect current evidence, evidence_analyst to reconcile comparison dimensions, and paper_author to draft the final comparison brief. Use quality_supervisor for one pass to ensure the comparison answers the requested dimensions without unsupported claims.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "competitors": {
          "description": "Comma-separated competitor names or products",
          "default": ""
        },
        "dimensions": {
          "description": "Comparison dimensions",
          "default": "pricing, features, market-position"
        },
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Preferred output shape",
          "default": "competitive intelligence brief with comparison table and key differentiators"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "product and strategy stakeholders"
        }
      }
    },
    "content_creation": {
      "description": "Research and draft audience-ready content such as blog posts, newsletters, product copy, or documentation.",
      "task": "When research is required, use researcher to gather current source material first, then content_writer to produce the requested content in the right tone and format. Use quality_supervisor for one acceptance pass so the draft stays aligned with the audience, tone, and evidence base.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "researcher",
        "content_writer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "contentType": {
          "description": "Requested content type",
          "default": "blog-post"
        },
        "topic": {
          "description": "Topic or key message",
          "default": ""
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "general technical audience"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Tone or voice guidance",
          "default": "professional and engaging"
        },
        "requiresResearch": {
          "description": "Whether research should happen before drafting",
          "default": "yes"
        }
      }
    },
    "code_review": {
      "description": "Perform a structured review of recent code changes and produce a merge-readiness report.",
      "task": "Use diff_reviewer to inspect the requested change set, code_analyst when a suspicious hunk needs deeper code-at-rest context, and qa_guard for one verification-oriented pass on missing tests or risky follow-ups. Produce a concise review report with concrete risks, missing tests, and a merge-readiness verdict.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "diff_reviewer",
        "code_analyst",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref range to review",
          "default": "HEAD~10..HEAD"
        },
        "focusAreas": {
          "description": "Review focus areas",
          "default": "all"
        }
      }
    },
    "reviewed_deliverable": {
      "description": "Build a deliverable and put it through an agent reviewer PANEL — parallel correctness + completeness + safety reviews with one bounded revision pass — before shipping. Review-by-default for ANY artifact (report, plan, app, config), not just code. Use when correctness matters more than speed.",
      "task": "Build and ship a REVIEWED deliverable for: {{request}}.\n\n1) BUILD: record_plan with the acceptance criteria the result must meet, then delegate to the right builder — content_writer for documents/reports/decks/static sites, backend_coder or web_coder for running apps (the builder must verify_app before reporting done), coder for scripts.\n2) REVIEW PANEL — run these reviewers IN PARALLEL with parallel_delegate, each scoped to ONE dimension and each returning a verdict (PASS or BLOCK) plus a SHORT list of must-fix items only: code_analyst for correctness/accuracy of the built artifact (use source_verifier INSTEAD when the deliverable makes sourced factual claims); quality_supervisor for completeness against the acceptance criteria; policy_compliance_reviewer for safety/security/policy. Instruct each reviewer to BLOCK only on genuine defects, not style preferences.\n3) REVISE (at most ONE pass): if any reviewer returned BLOCK, delegate ONE revision to the ORIGINAL builder addressing EVERY must-fix item together, then re-check with only the reviewer(s) that blocked. Do not loop more than once — if issues remain after one revision, ship the best version with the open items called out explicitly.\n4) SHIP: return the deliverable plus a 2-3 line review summary (dimensions checked, what each reviewer found, what was fixed). Never ship an artifact that failed the correctness reviewer without flagging the unresolved risk.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "content_writer",
        "backend_coder",
        "web_coder",
        "coder",
        "code_analyst",
        "source_verifier",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "request": {
          "description": "The deliverable to build and review (subject + desired shape)",
          "default": "the deliverable described in the conversation"
        },
        "reviewDimensions": {
          "description": "Which review dimensions to emphasize",
          "default": "correctness, completeness, safety"
        }
      }
    },
    "verified_app_build": {
      "description": "Build a runnable browser app or game — a playable game, an interactive canvas page, a small web tool — and prove it works by EXECUTING it before reporting done. Use this whenever the deliverable is something the user will open and interact with. It is the generate -> test -> fix loop made explicit, because a build that is only read is a build that is only hoped for: every measured failure of this pipeline shipped a file that looked finished and died on its first line.",
      "task": "Build a WORKING app for: {{request}}.\n\nThis scene exists because reading code is not evidence that it runs. Every measured failure of this pipeline shipped a file that looked finished and died on its first line, and every one would have been caught by executing it once. So the test step is not optional and not delegable to an agent that only reads.\n\n1) BUILD: delegate to web_coder (browser/canvas/DOM apps) or backend_coder (apps needing a server). Large builds are staged: one small skeleton whose unbuilt parts are single UNFINISHED_STUB lines that throw, then ONE edit_file per stub filling it with complete code. Never re-emit the whole file to change part of it.\n\n2) RUN IT: the builder MUST call verify_page on the built HTML before saying it is done. verify_page executes every script against a DOM and reports the exact error with its line, the source, and a caret under the column - plus where the page painted on each canvas, because a drawing that lands outside its canvas runs perfectly and shows the user nothing.\n\n3) FIX AND RE-RUN: on FAIL, fix the FIRST error it names (the later ones are usually consequences of it) and call verify_page again. Repeat until it passes. Do NOT delegate this to a reviewer to READ the file - a reader cannot tell a working page from a broken one, and the error already says where the fault is. If two fixes in a row do not move the verdict, change approach rather than repeating the edit.\n\n4) ONLY THEN REPORT: state the artifact path and what verify_page returned. If it still fails, say so plainly with the outstanding error - never describe a page as playable while its own check fails.\n\nquality_supervisor is available for a completeness pass against the request AFTER the page runs, never instead of running it.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "web_coder",
        "backend_coder",
        "coder",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "expectArtifact": true,
      "params": {
        "request": {
          "description": "The app or game to build (what it is, and how the user will use it)",
          "default": "the app described in the conversation"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
